  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates an electrochemical process that removes carbon dioxide from a piperazine-based solvent using a membrane electrode assembly. On the left, a CO2-rich piperazine solution enters the device containing carbon-bearing species, while the outlet stream is shown as a CO2-lean piperazine solution, indicating regeneration of the solvent. At the same time, carbon monoxide is produced as a separate product on the right, suggesting that captured CO2 is being converted rather than simply released.\n\nThe central drawing emphasizes the reactor hardware: a compact electrochemical cell with flow ports and layered internal components characteristic of a membrane electrode assembly. The overall message is that absorbed CO2 in the amine solution can be directly processed inside this cell to yield CO and a regenerated capture medium.\n\nThe panel on the right zooms in on the catalytic surface and appears to show a molecule containing carbon and oxygen interacting with a nickel-containing, nitrogen-doped carbon material. The legend identifies Ni, N, C, O, and H atoms. This close-up supports the mechanistic idea that the catalyst surface promotes conversion of the CO2-derived species into carbon monoxide at the electrode interface.",
